---
import architecture from '../assets/wristband-architecture.png';
import design from '../assets/wristband-design.png';
import Plate from './Plate.astro';

const parts = [
  {
    name: 'Multispectral sensor',
    role: 'Captures optical wavelength bands. Whether the extra bands help recognize unreliable readings is the research question of this project.',
    tag: 'Experimental',
  },
  { name: 'PPG sensor', role: 'Captures the pulse wave, from which heart rate is estimated.', tag: null },
  {
    name: 'Skin-temperature sensor',
    role: 'Tracks the trend of skin temperature. It is not core body temperature.',
    tag: null,
  },
  { name: 'Accelerometer', role: 'Detects movement and motion artifacts, so affected readings can be set aside.', tag: null },
  { name: 'Microcontroller and radio', role: 'Collects the readings and transmits them locally to the onboard server.', tag: null },
  { name: 'Battery and power management', role: 'Powers sensing on the wrist.', tag: null },
];
---
<section class="section" id="wristband">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / Wristband</p>
  <h2 class="section-title">Screenless sensing. Continuous contact.</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">
    The wristband has one job: keep its sensors against the skin and send what they read to
    the onboard server.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no screen and nothing to operate. A titanium outer shell and a soft inner liner
    hold a single sensor module on the inner face of the band. The band measures; it does
    not interpret. Analysis happens on the server, a short wireless hop away.
  </p>

  <Plate
    image={architecture}
    variant="render"
    title="Wristband: sensor architecture"
    alt="Exploded view of the wristband in three layers: the outer shell, the electronics carrier and the inner liner with its sensor module. Six numbered components are described in the list that follows."
  >
    Exploded view. Proposed component layout.
  </Plate>

  <ol class="parts">
    {parts.map((part, i) => (
      <li>
        <p class="part-index">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</p>
        <h3>{part.name}{part.tag && <span class="tag">{part.tag}</span>}</h3>
        <p>{part.role}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <p>
    A multispectral sensor measures the spectral content of light. That alone does not make
    it a validated measure of glucose, hydration or disease, and Spectra does not claim it is.
  </p>

  <Plate
    image={design}
    variant="blueprint"
    title="Wristband: design study"
    alt="Design study of the wristband with top, side, inner-face and isometric views, and a section through the sensor module."
  >
    Design study: top, side, inner-face and isometric views, with section A-A through the
    sensor module. Called out: titanium outer shell, soft inner liner, optical sensor window,
    skin-temperature contact, embedded electronics and concealed clasp.
  </Plate>
</section>
